<Article slug="expiration-and-eviction">
<p>At noon, our application saves a product’s £12 price for six seconds. One second later, the shop changes the price to £15. A visitor arrives at three seconds and sees £12. Another arrives at six seconds.</p>
<p>What should happen to that second visitor? The answer depends on what we mean by “for six seconds”. Six seconds after loading the price is one policy. Six seconds after anyone last used it is another. Both can keep a cache busy; only the first necessarily stops this particular old copy being reused.</p>
<p>Move time past six seconds below. Then enable renewal on every hit and replay the same requests.</p>
<Expiry />
<p>With a fixed six-second lifetime, the request at six seconds misses and loads £15. With renewal, the request at three seconds moves the expiry to nine; the request at six moves it to twelve. The entry remains popular, so it remains available. Its contents are still the price loaded at zero.</p>

<h2>Time to live and sliding expiration</h2>
<p>A <strong>time to live</strong>, or <strong>TTL</strong>, gives an entry an expiry deadline. Ordinary Redis <code>GET</code> calls do not extend that deadline. Saving a value with <code>SET key value EX 6</code> gives it a six-second lifetime from that write, regardless of how often it is read.</p>
<p>Renewing the deadline on access implements <strong>sliding expiration</strong>. Redis’s <code>GETEX key EX 6</code> can return a string and give it another six seconds. This can express an idle timeout, but an idle timeout and a freshness limit answer different questions. “Nobody has used this recently” says nothing about whether its source has changed.</p>
<p>In the experiment, set the TTL to three seconds. Every read now arrives at the deadline, so even sliding expiration cannot preserve the entry: it has expired before the read can renew it. Set it to twelve and compare the source-read counts. The effect of a TTL depends on the spacing of requests as well as its numeric value.</p>
<p>If the application needs both an idle limit and a maximum age, track both explicitly. For example, keep a deadline based on the last trustworthy source observation and never let an idle extension move past it. A successful cache hit is not a new observation of the source.</p>
<p>Expiration also does not fetch a replacement. After a key expires, a cache-aside application waits for a request to miss and reload it. If nobody asks, there is no reason to perform that work. If many callers ask together, several may try; the stampede article will examine that case.</p>

<h2>Set the value and its expiry together</h2>
<p>For a string that should expire, put the value and the lifetime in the same command:</p>
<pre><code>{`SET card:v1:harbour:42 '{"price":12}' EX 60
TTL card:v1:harbour:42
# A non-negative number of seconds remaining`}</code></pre>
<p>Using separate <code>SET</code> and <code>EXPIRE</code> commands leaves a failure point between them. If the application stops after the first command, the new entry has no expiry. A single <code>SET</code> with an expiry option avoids that particular gap. It does not make a preceding database read part of a Redis transaction.</p>
<p>Replacement is another opportunity to lose a deadline. Plain <code>SET</code> clears an existing expiry. <code>SET … KEEPTTL</code> preserves it, while <code>SET … EX 60</code> starts a new sixty-second lifetime. Choose deliberately: preserving the deadline can make a newly replaced value disappear soon, whereas resetting it can extend the life of data that was already old.</p>
<p><code>TTL</code> returns <code>-1</code> for an existing key with no expiry and <code>-2</code> for a missing key. Neither means “almost expired”. <code>PTTL</code> reports the remaining time in milliseconds. These commands help inspect the policy actually attached to a key, rather than the policy a writer was supposed to attach.</p>
<p>Redis detects expired keys when they are accessed and also removes expired keys through background work. An entry becoming unavailable and its memory being reclaimed are related events, not a promise that a timer callback runs exactly at your chosen instant. Do not use cache expiration as a precise job scheduler.</p>

<h2>A TTL is not a complete freshness guarantee</h2>
<p>Our illustration quietly made a strong assumption: a miss reads current source data and saves it immediately. Real loading takes time. Suppose a loader observes £12, spends four seconds assembling the response, then saves it with a six-second TTL. That response can be almost ten seconds removed from the source observation when Redis finally stops serving it.</p>
<FreshnessBudget />
<p>A replica can add more delay before the loader even starts. Another cache can supply an already-aged answer. A slow earlier reader can save an old result after a newer write. A six-second Redis TTL bounds the lifetime of that particular Redis entry after its write; it does not, by itself, bound all the age accumulated before the write.</p>
<p>Start with the application’s requirement. “The catalogue may lag a committed update by at most thirty seconds” is different from “a cached entry lives for thirty seconds”. To establish the first, account for source visibility, loading time, cache lifetime and any further copies. If those delays have usable bounds, allocate the freshness budget across them. If they do not, a TTL alone cannot prove the requirement.</p>
<p>Store a source revision or observation time when it helps validate that contract, but do not mistake a timestamp added at cache insertion for the time the source state was observed. Cross-machine time comparisons also need an explicit clock assumption. For a purchase or another consequential transition, rechecking the authoritative state may be more appropriate than trusting the display cache.</p>
<p>Once the acceptable freshness is clear, measure the workload inside that limit. Shorter TTLs tend to discard more opportunities for reuse; longer ones permit a changed source to remain hidden for longer. Neither “five minutes everywhere” nor “as long as possible” is a substitute for deciding what a stale answer would do to this application.</p>

<h2>Expiration and eviction solve different problems</h2>
<p>Now imagine every cached price has fifty seconds left, but Redis has no room for another product card. Waiting for expiry is not enough. Something must give: discard an existing entry, reject the new write, or provide more capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Eviction</strong> removes an entry to satisfy a memory policy. It can remove a fresh entry long before its TTL. <strong>Expiration</strong> makes an entry unavailable because its deadline has passed, even when plenty of memory remains. A cache hit needs both conditions: the entry has not expired and it has survived the other reasons it might disappear.</p>
<p>In a reproducible Redis 8.2.2 experiment, we wrote one hundred values with ten-minute TTLs into a small cache using <code>allkeys-lru</code>. Redis reported evictions while its expired-key count remained zero. The lifetime had not reserved any memory. Your miss path must work even for a key that was saved a moment ago.</p>
<p>Redis’s <code>maxmemory</code> setting and <code>maxmemory-policy</code> control this capacity decision. They are not a complete process-memory budget: reserve room for memory outside eviction accounting and for the operating conditions discussed in the memory article. Setting the limit to all the RAM available to the process leaves no such margin.</p>
<p>Before choosing a policy, it helps to see what it is trying to predict.</p>

<h2>LRU, LFU and the working set</h2>
<p>Suppose we can keep two equally sized entries. Requests repeatedly use A and B, then briefly ask for C and D before returning. Should those recent newcomers displace the established pair?</p>
<p><strong>Least recently used</strong>, or <strong>LRU</strong>, discards the entry whose last access is oldest. <strong>Least frequently used</strong>, or <strong>LFU</strong>, favours entries with more recorded accesses. Step through the same request sequence under both policies.</p>
<Eviction />
<p>During the initial A/B requests, both policies save the same work. The difference appears when C and D arrive. LRU follows the newest activity and loses the old pair. The simple LFU model retains one frequently used entry through the scan, so one of the returning requests hits.</p>
<p>Now choose “The hot pair changes”. C and D are no longer a brief interruption: they are the new useful data. Old frequency counts can keep an obsolete favourite in the cache while new requests compete for the remaining space. Recent use and past frequency are evidence about future demand, not knowledge of it.</p>
<p>Redis makes these decisions approximately. Its LRU policy samples candidates instead of maintaining the exact global order shown here. Its LFU policy uses probabilistic counters that decay so past popularity can lose influence. Treat the experiment as a way to understand the competing signals, not a prediction of which exact key Redis will evict next.</p>
<p>Choose the three-key cycle with two slots. Every request misses under the LRU model: by the time A returns, B and C have displaced it. Increase capacity to three and, after the first three loads, every request hits. We crossed a <strong>working set</strong> boundary: the entries needed again soon can now coexist.</p>
<p>This explains why the size of the entire database is often the wrong sizing target. A much smaller active subset can serve most requests. It also explains why an average hit rate can conceal a damaging workload: a one-off scan can replace the active subset with entries that will never be reused. Look at access order and reuse distance, not just a list of popular keys.</p>
<p>A request trace is useful evidence, but it describes the traffic it captured. Repeat the comparison for peak load, changing popularity, tenant skew and deployment cold starts. A policy that wins on yesterday’s catalogue may lose when the front page promotes a different set of products.</p>

<h2>Admission policy and byte capacity</h2>
<p>Entries are rarely the same size. A small product card and a large search response each count as one hit, but they consume very different amounts of memory. Making room for one large answer can discard several small ones.</p>
<p>The next cache holds eight allocation units. A, B and C each occupy two; the large response L occupies six. Read the three cards, insert L, then return to the cards.</p>
<Admission />
<p>Admitting L first removes A and B. Returning to A removes C; later requests continue reloading. In this seven-request trace every request misses. Bypassing L’s cache fill preserves all three cards, so their second reads hit. The request for L still receives its source answer; we simply decline to store it.</p>
<p>An <strong>admission policy</strong> decides what enters a cache. An eviction policy decides what leaves it. Applying a size limit before a fill is one possible admission rule. Requiring evidence of repeated use is another, though tracking that evidence itself costs space and work. These are application choices; enabling an LRU eviction policy does not automatically reject every low-value newcomer.</p>
<p>Do not turn the example into a rule that large values are bad. If L were frequently reused, or exceptionally costly to rebuild, storing it could save more work than all three cards. Compare expected reuse, miss cost and occupied bytes. Then include the cost of transferring and decoding the saved representation. Optimising the number of hits alone can choose the wrong entries.</p>
<p>The eight units in the diagram are deliberately abstract. In Redis, keys, values, metadata and allocation overhead all consume memory. Measure representative entries and workload behaviour rather than multiplying a JSON payload’s length by the number of keys and calling the result a capacity plan.</p>

<h2>Choose eviction eligibility as well as a ranking</h2>
<p>The policy name also determines which keys Redis may consider. In our Redis 8.2 baseline, <code>allkeys-lru</code> and <code>allkeys-lfu</code> consider the whole keyspace. Their <code>volatile-</code> counterparts consider only keys with an expiry. <code>volatile-ttl</code> favours eligible keys with shorter remaining lifetimes; random policies provide another ranking. A TTL on a key does not mean it must survive until that deadline under any of these eviction policies.</p>
<p>With <code>noeviction</code>, Redis rejects memory-growing operations when the configured limit prevents them, rather than evicting entries to admit them. In our worksheet, a new <code>SET</code> failed with an <code>OOM</code> reply while an existing value remained readable. A cache fill failure must be handled as a possible result; a successful source read does not guarantee that saving its answer will succeed.</p>
<p>The worksheet also puts <code>volatile-lru</code> under pressure with only a non-expiring key present. There is no eligible victim, so the new fill fails. Forgetting TTLs can therefore change both freshness and capacity behaviour. Inspect the keys and the effective configuration when a supposedly evicting cache starts rejecting writes.</p>
<p>A dedicated cache containing reconstructible data makes the ownership rule clear: any entry may disappear. Mixing cache copies with irreplaceable records on one instance makes an <code>allkeys-</code> policy dangerous, while a <code>volatile-</code> policy does not reserve capacity for future cache fills. Separate workloads when they need different loss and resource guarantees.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/expiration-and-eviction.sh" download>expiration and eviction worksheet</a> runs these cases in its own isolated Redis 8.2.2 container and removes it afterwards. With Docker running, save it and run <code>sh expiration-and-eviction.sh</code>. It also verifies <code>GET</code>, <code>KEEPTTL</code>, plain replacement, <code>GETEX</code> and expiry replies. All assertions were executed during preparation; exact memory counts and eviction victims are deliberately not asserted.</p>

<h2>Diagnose why reuse is being lost</h2>
<p>When hit rate falls, first separate the causes. Are requests asking for new keys? Are reusable entries reaching their deadlines? Are still-useful entries being evicted? Are fills being rejected or bypassed? Those possibilities call for different changes.</p>
<p>Compare changes in Redis’s <code>expired_keys</code> and <code>evicted_keys</code> counters over the affected interval, alongside application hit, miss and fill outcomes. A high expiration count is not automatically bad: cold entries also need cleanup. A high eviction count can be normal turnover. The question is whether entries are being removed before useful reuse, and what work those misses create downstream.</p>
<p>Longer TTLs will not fix a working set that does not fit. More memory will not make an overlong freshness policy acceptable. A better ranking cannot create reuse in unique requests. Choose the freshness contract, decide what is worth admitting, and size and test the resulting working set.</p>
<p>Even then, another problem remains. A request may read the source before an update and fill the cache after it. Expiration eventually removes that copy, but cannot prevent the race. <a href="/learn/databases/caching/#collection">The collection continues with cache invalidation and consistency</a>.</p>

